# T-0021: the git blob for this fixture is LF (1831 bytes), but a Windows
# checkout with core.autocrlf=true silently re-inflates it to CRLF (1882
# bytes) in the working tree, changing its sha256 out from under
# tests/test_profile.py — which hashes the working-tree file, not the blob.
# That made the test pass on Windows and fail on a Linux clone. Force LF on
# checkout everywhere so the hash this test asserts is the same on both.
tests/fixtures/online_retail_ii_sample.csv text eol=lf

# T-0021: same autocrlf trap, wider blast radius. `results/*.csv` and
# `results/*.md` are written by the eval harness with newline="\n" (LF), so
# the git blob is LF, but a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true was
# still re-inflating some of them to CRLF in the working tree — enough to
# make the "regenerated results/ are byte-identical to committed, at seed
# 42" claim in CLAUDE.md false on a fresh Linux clone even though the
# generated *numbers* matched. Force LF for every generated results/ text
# artifact so the working tree always matches what the writers actually
# wrote, regardless of platform or checkout history. results/figures/*.png
# is binary and intentionally excluded.
results/*.md text eol=lf
results/*.csv text eol=lf
results/*.json text eol=lf
